  "artPrint": {
    "id": 8257630503001,
    "title": "Forest Sketch",
    "handle": "forest-sketch",
    "body_html": "<p><strong>Tones:</strong> Graphite, Parchment<br></p>\n<p><span><strong>About The Print: </strong>A forest sketch in black and white of a treeline lining a grassy meadow. Dry point etching on paper.    </span></p>\n<p><span><strong>About The Artist: </strong>Print by Alexander Shilling after the artist Dwight William Tryon. Tryon was an American landscape painter known for his serene and atmospheric depictions of the New England countryside. He initially pursued a career in engraving before devoting himself fully to painting. His work often featured soft, harmonious compositions with a focus on light, mood, and the effects of atmosphere. His tranquil landscapes and signature tonalist style, characterized by their subtle color palettes, earned him recognition as one of the leading American landscape painters of the late 19th and early 20th centuries. Alexander Schilling was not only a landscape painter and etcher but also known for his skill in creating prints based on the works of other artists. This practice, common in the 19th and early 20th centuries, involved translating original paintings into etchings or engravings, allowing broader public access to art through printed reproductions. Schilling's interpretations retained the mood and composition of the originals while showcasing his technical finesse in line and tone.<br>His printmaking work contributed to preserving and disseminating notable artworks before the widespread use of photographic reproduction.</span></p>",
    "vendor": "Vintage",
    "product_type": "Horizontal",
